namespace SynthEBD;

// 3-part documentation for the BodySlide Compare window (Window_BodySlideCompare): the
// per-pane preset / NPC / weight inputs and the cross-pane camera-lock and superimpose
// controls. Opened from the "Compare" button on any OBody-menu CharacterViewer toolbar
// (documented under CharacterViewer.Compare).
public static partial class UiDocs
{
    private static void RegisterBodySlideCompare()
    {
        // ---------- Per-pane inputs ----------

        Add("BodySlideCompare.BodyType",
            layperson: "Which body (CBBE 3BA, BHUNP, HIMBO, ...) this side shows. Bodies whose mods you have installed but switched off in your mod manager are offered too. It decides which presets the list below offers, and whether the NPC box offers male or female NPCs. The two sides are independent, so you can put the same kind of preset on two different bodies. A body you don't have installed in-game is shown with the mesh BodySlide builds it from; your NPC's skin textures don't fit another body's layout, so it wears that body's own textures if its mod ships them, and plain beige skin otherwise. The note next to the box says which you're looking at.",
            technical: "Options are Body Type Registry entries with a BodySlide source mesh on disk (BodyTypeSourceMeshes): a slider set in the entry's slider-set fingerprints (.osp, or an older .xml under SliderSets) whose OutputFile is the gender's main body (femalebody / malebody, or a suffixed form), vanilla character-assets output first; its ShapeData DataFolder (older: SetFolder) \\ SourceFile is the mesh, and its sliders' Data / datafile entries give each slider's data and target shape, loaded regrouped per target shape (SynthEbdOsdLoader.LoadFiles), with the preset turned into the values BodySlide would build with (BodySlideBuildRules: the set's default for any slider the preset doesn't list -- so hidden sliders such as 3BA NeckSeam / BHUNP Neck, which narrow the neck at weight 0 to meet the head, take effect -- then 1 - value for inverted sliders; zap / UV / clamp sliders dropped). Without that a source mesh shows a neck gap below weight 100. Fallback: the .nif beside a registry ShapeData .osd. Searched in the merged Data folder first, then in each inactive mod (ISourceResolver.GetInactiveModFolders: MO2 profile's disabled mods by priority; Vortex staging mods with nothing deployed), whose own folder then also supplies the textures. Selecting one sets the pane's gender, rebuilds the preset list to presets whose SliderGroup matches, and resets the preview NPC when the gender changes. The installed type (a candidate source mesh shares the worn body's largest shape name and vertex count) renders the NPC's real worn body with its .tri. Any other type sets VM_CharacterViewer.BodyReplacement to the source mesh, deforms it with that set's OSDs (SetBodyMorphOsdOverride) and takes as its TextureFolder the mod that ships the mesh (ISourceResolver.FindProvidingModFolder: MO2's highest-priority enabled mod, or the Vortex staging folder its vortex.deployment.json names); the body's skin shapes then use the NIF's own texture paths found in that folder, or NeutralSkinTexture beige when the diffuse isn't there (always, with no mod manager configured). The NPC's hands, feet and tail are swapped too when the body type's slider sets build that mesh path (BodyReplacement.PartMeshes, from each set's OutputPath\\OutputFile; e.g. BHUNP's hands and feet), and every body part's skin shapes take the same skin, so there is no seam at the wrists or ankles; only the body is morphed. The face's skin follows as well, slot by slot, wherever the chosen skin's folder has the texture the NPC's head NIF asks for (VM_CharacterViewer.ApplyReplacementHeadTextures: diffuse, normal, subsurface, specular, detail / complexion map; the FaceGen tint stays the NPC's), so the neck matches; an NPC with custom face textures keeps them. Opens on the body type of the menu that opened the window.",
            motivation: "Different bodies react differently to the same slider values; seeing them side by side, or both zeroed, shows what each body starts from.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.SkinSource",
            layperson: "Whose skin textures this body type wears here. Some bodies share a skin layout, so skins made for one fit the other: CBBE wears CBBE 3BA skins, UNP wears BHUNP skins. Pick the body whose skins fit; if that is the body you have installed, this side wears your NPC's real skin. Your choice is remembered for this body type, in both panes and next time. It has no effect on the body you have installed, which always wears your NPC's skin. With a mod manager set up, 'From a mod...' lets you pick any installed mod's skin instead (see the Mod box). Each choice says what you will get: 'your NPC's skin', that body's own skin, or 'none included - beige' when that body's mod doesn't ship its skin texture (many body mods leave skins to separate texture mods).",
            technical: "Persisted as Settings_OBody.CompareSkinSourceByBodyType (body type -> skin body type; Own skin removes the key), edited live through VM_SettingsOBody and shared by both panes (CompareSkinMap). Mappings chain (A -> B -> C) and stop before a cycle. For a body type shown through its source mesh, the resolved skin body type decides the textures: the installed type -> BodyReplacement.UsesNpcSkin (the NPC's own skin TXST and tint, as on the worn body); any other type -> BodyReplacement.TextureFolder = the mod folder shipping that type's source mesh, where the replaced NIF's own texture paths are looked up (so the mapping works when the two bodies use the same texture file names, as UV-compatible bodies do); not found -> neutral beige. Option labels predict this off the UI thread (RefreshSkinOptionLabels): an option's mapping chain is resolved, then judged installed -> \"your NPC's skin\", BodyTypeSourceMeshes.HasOwnSkin (the main ShaderType-5 shape's NIF diffuse, found in the body's mod folder by VM_CharacterViewer.ResolveTextureUnderFolder, the renderer's own rule) -> plain name, else \"none included - beige\".",
            motivation: "Skin textures are drawn on a body's UV layout, so they only render correctly on a body with the same layout. Without the mapping a body that has no textures of its own falls back to plain beige even when a compatible skin is right there.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.SkinMod",
            layperson: "Shown when Skin is set to 'From a mod...': pick an installed mod whose skin this body should wear here, such as a UNP skin texture mod for BHUNP. Only mods that contain the skin texture file this body asks for are listed, whether or not they are switched on in your mod manager. Whether a listed skin really fits the body is up to you; a skin made for a different body layout will look scrambled. Your choice is remembered for this body type.",
            technical: "Needs mod manager integration (ISourceResolver.GetAllModFolders: MO2 enabled then disabled mods by priority; Vortex staging mods, deployed first). Options are mods where the diffuse of the body's source mesh's main ShaderType-5 shape resolves by VM_CharacterViewer.ResolveTextureUnderFolder (BodyTypeSourceMeshes.FindModsWithSkin, searched off the UI thread each time the picker opens). Persisted as Settings_OBody.CompareSkinModByBodyType (body type -> mod folder name), which replaces the body type's skin body-type mapping and vice versa. A body-type chain stops at a body type with a mod, and that mod applies. The chosen folder becomes BodyReplacement.TextureFolder; a mod that is gone renders neutral beige and is still listed so the saved choice is visible.",
            motivation: "Most body mods ship no skin of their own; compatible skins come in separate texture mods, often ones you keep switched off because only one skin can be active in-game.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.PresetFilter",
            layperson: "Type here to narrow the preset dropdown below to names containing what you typed.",
            technical: "Case-insensitive substring filter over the pane's preset list, re-applied on every keystroke. The Zeroed entry always stays. The current selection is preserved when it still passes the filter, so typing never blanks the viewer underneath.",
            motivation: "Preset libraries routinely run to hundreds of entries; scrolling an unfiltered dropdown to find one body type is slow.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.Preset",
            layperson: "The BodySlide preset shown on this side. The first entry, Zeroed, shows the body with every slider at zero, which is handy for comparing bodies on their own terms. The list is already narrowed by the Filter box above, and you can type in the dropdown to jump to a name.",
            technical: "Bound to VM_BodySlideComparePane.SelectedPreset over FilteredPresets, with WPF TextSearch on the preset Label. Zeroed is a synthetic BodySlideSetting like BodySlide's own Zeroed Sliders: every visible shape slider of the body type's slider set at 0 (hidden sliders keep their set defaults), the pane's body type as SliderGroup; it stays selected when the body type changes. Selecting one applies its sliders to the pane's viewer at the pane's current weight, generation-guarded so rapid changes can't land a stale preset over a newer one.",
            motivation: "Choosing the two presets is the whole point of the window; everything else on the pane exists to make that comparison fair.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.Weight",
            layperson: "The NPC weight (0-100) this side previews at. Changing it re-shapes the body, and it also narrows the NPC box below to NPCs at that weight.",
            technical: "Sets VM_BodySlideComparePane.Weight, which re-applies the preset's weight interpolation and, on a 400 ms throttle, re-runs PreviewNpcResolver.FindNpcsAtWeight off-thread to rebuild the NPC picker's candidate list. The throttle matters because that scan walks every NPC record in the load order, so dragging the spinner costs one scan rather than one per value. Half of the weight-NPC interlock: this direction restricts which NPCs are offered.",
            motivation: "BodySlide presets interpolate between a weight-0 and a weight-100 shape, so two presets can look alike at one weight and quite different at another - comparing at a chosen weight is essential.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.PreviewNpc",
            layperson: "The NPC this side is previewed on. The dropdown lists NPCs at the weight set above, but you can also type or paste any other NPC - and doing so moves the Weight box to that NPC's own weight. It fills itself in on open with whoever is being previewed by default.",
            technical: "The picker's CandidateFormKeys is bound to the weight-filtered candidate list, which narrows its suggestions only: the display of the current FormKey still resolves against the full record index, and a typed or pasted parseable FormKey is accepted outright. That escape hatch is what makes the second half of the interlock reachable - setting this reads the record's Weight via PreviewNpcResolver.GetNpcWeight and snaps the pane's Weight to it (rounded), guarded so the change doesn't bounce back and reset the NPC. When nothing is picked the pane resolves a default from the OBody Misc per-weight table at the nearest configured slot and writes it back here, so the box names who is actually on screen rather than sitting empty.",
            motivation: "Restricting the list to the chosen weight keeps the comparison fair, but a preview box that disagreed with the model on screen - or that refused an NPC you named - would be worse than no restriction at all.");

        // ---------- Cross-pane controls ----------

        Add("BodySlideCompare.LockCamera",
            layperson: "Keeps both previews pointing the same way: rotate, zoom or pan either side and the other follows.",
            technical: "Mirrors the five view-defining OrbitCamera properties (azimuth, elevation, distance, target, field of view) between the panes via OrbitCamera.CopyViewFrom, which suppresses the receiving camera's change event so the two cannot echo each other. Because the event is raised from the property setters rather than the mouse handlers, an automatic reframe after an NPC load propagates too.",
            motivation: "Two bodies viewed from even slightly different angles cannot be compared reliably - perspective alone changes apparent proportions.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.Superimpose",
            layperson: "Draws pane B's model inside pane A's preview, on top of A's own model, so you can see exactly where the two shapes differ. Pane B keeps showing its own view as well.",
            technical: "Loads pane B's NPC and preset as a guest scene inside pane A's viewer. The guest goes through pane A's own mesh builder, texture manager and GL context - GL objects cannot be shared between two GLWpfControls - and is installed into a separate mesh list that the primary scene's bookkeeping never touches. The overlay survives pane A changing its own preset: the primary reload tears the guest meshes down and it re-installs once the new scene commits.",
            motivation: "Side-by-side comparison shows that two bodies differ; overlaying them shows where, which is what you need in order to act on it.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.SuperimposeStyle",
            layperson: "How the superimposed model is drawn. Translucent shows it as a see-through colored body, Wireframe as coloured outlines only, and Textured draws it fully like the model underneath.",
            technical: "Applied per-mesh with no extra render pass. Translucent sets a flat tint plus sub-1 MaterialAlpha with depth writes off, so it sorts into the alpha-blend pass and never occludes the primary model. Wireframe reuses the missing-texture fallback's draw behavior (solid passes skipped, edges only) with a per-mesh color override, so it does not get mistaken for the green that means a texture failed to decode. Textured leaves the mesh exactly as the texture pass built it.",
            motivation: "Which one reads best depends on the comparison: translucency shows volume differences, wireframe never hides the model underneath, and textured is the honest full-detail view.");

        Add("BodySlideCompare.SuperimposeBodyOnly",
            layperson: "Overlays only the body of pane B, leaving out its head, hair and any worn gear. Usually what you want - two complete models at the same spot mostly obscure each other.",
            technical: "Filters the guest install to the Body part. Changing it re-installs from the retained load result, so it costs a GL upload but no NIF re-parse. Independent of the style setting, so all six style/scope combinations are reachable.",
            motivation: "BodySlide sliders only move the body; heads and hair drawn twice at one origin add nothing to the comparison and a lot of visual noise.");
    }
}
